a pincer movement philippinoto met each other at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.480
the bridge bevin not far from the city of lille&nbsp;
on the modern day french belgium border it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.440
fierce battle for by heavily armored soldiers&nbsp;
hacking each other with swords maces and axes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:09.920
typical for the high middle ages after hours&nbsp;
of fighting the critical moment came when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:14.240
french king and the german emperor clashed in&nbsp;
the center of the field with their retinues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:18.880
with both men nearly being killed and having&nbsp;
horses killed from underneath them after nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.040
being captured by the french knights otto was&nbsp;
forced to flee the battlefield his deputies the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:28.400
counts of flanders and bologna were not so lucky&nbsp;
and were taken prisoner the imperial army had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:34.400
decisively defeated the loss at bouvoin spelled&nbsp;
the end of john's ambitions in france left without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:38.800
allies he was forced to sue for peace and once&nbsp;
again dejectedly returned to england empty-handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:44.240
by now things were really reaching a breaking&nbsp;
point the nobles of england were on the precipice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:49.040
of rebellion the discontent between john and&nbsp;
his subjects had been building for years john's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:53.680
oppressive taxes were making life difficult for&nbsp;
more and more people commoner and noble alike the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:57.280
fact that all of this money was being squandered&nbsp;
on campaigns against the french king that failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:03.360
to accomplish anything just made things worse&nbsp;
there were also serious questions being raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:08.240
about jon's behavior while it was expected for&nbsp;
wealthy men at the time to keep mistresses it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:13.120
was considered unacceptable for those mistresses&nbsp;
to include married noble women something jon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:17.920
known to have done several times for that matter&nbsp;
his wife isabella of all joules might have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:24.160
as young as nine years old when she was married to&nbsp;
the king in 1199 and had already been betrothed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:30.560
another nobleman hugh of lucinor neither of which&nbsp;
apparently bothered john that much he treated most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:35.840
of his nobles with outright contempt preferring&nbsp;
the company of his toadies the new man that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:41.040
entrusted to help him run the government instead&nbsp;
of the land in ability and in a move that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:46.480
serious political and social consequences he had&nbsp;
embarked on a dispute with pope innocent iii that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.920
had ended with his excommunication in 1209. the&nbsp;
pope also put the entirety of england under papal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:56.880
interdict which forbade the saying of mass inside&nbsp;
any church in the country at a time when almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:03.760
all of society revolved around the church this&nbsp;
was a serious blow the two had reconciled in 1213&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:09.040
but the experience still haunted the pious kingdom&nbsp;
wide and served to turn the majority of england's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:15.120
clergy against the king with so many enemies and&nbsp;
so few friends rebellion was probably inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:19.440
it finally flared up in the aftermath of john's&nbsp;
failed campaign in france in 1214. a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:24.240
coalition of barons from the north of england&nbsp;
banded together in april 1215 in the city of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:29.120
northampton and at the head of a large body of men&nbsp;
marched south capturing the city of london john's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:34.000
position was clearly untenable and in a last-ditch&nbsp;
effort to save his crown he sued for peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:46.960
the king met with his rebellious barons on the&nbsp;
15th of june 1215 ronnie meet 20 miles west to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:51.280
london attempting to mediate between the two&nbsp;
was steven langton the archbishop of canterbury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:55.440
the negotiations went beyond the individual&nbsp;
demands of the nobles and raised questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:00.160
about the way kingdoms were governed up to now&nbsp;
no real checks on the absolute power of kings to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:04.480
do whatever they wanted existed either in england&nbsp;
or most anywhere in europe while english kings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:08.640
swore an oath at their coronation to rule in&nbsp;
accordance with the laws and customs that already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:14.160
existed in the kingdom there was no legal recourse&nbsp;
if a king simply chose not to do so it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:19.680
fatal flaw when a bad king like king john came&nbsp;
along put the entire system at risk of collapse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:23.040
in an attempt to rectify this what the&nbsp;
nobles wanted was a guarantee of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:27.760
rights and liberties under the law they wanted the&nbsp;
rights of the church to be respected by the king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:32.400
protection from illegal imprisonment and a fair&nbsp;
justice system that they could appeal to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:37.920
they especially wanted was the establishments of&nbsp;
limits on the king's ability to tax his subjects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:43.600
without their consent the two sides agreed to the&nbsp;
great charter or magna carta in latin on the 19th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:48.320
of june the charter called for the creation of a&nbsp;
body of 25 nobles who were empowered to coerce jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:52.800
into complying with the terms of the charter they&nbsp;
would ensure that the king ruled not according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:58.720
his own personal whims but according to the law of&nbsp;
the land most historians consider the creation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:04.160
this legislative body to be the first english&nbsp;
parliament neither side really believed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.880
the peace agreement would hold for long this was&nbsp;
proved out when it defined john appealed to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:13.600
one-time adversary pope innocent for help as part&nbsp;
of john's peace agreement with the pope he agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:18.480
to make england a papal vassal state and innocent&nbsp;
believed that the magna carta curtailed his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:23.840
rights as a feudal lord so he declared the charter&nbsp;
to be null and void it became clear to the barons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:28.960
that if they wanted their rights guaranteed by law&nbsp;
they'd first have to earn them on the battlefield

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:43.040
the entire kingdom descended into civil war in&nbsp;
1215. hard-pressed by john the rebel barons turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:48.320
to france for help king philip proved reluctant&nbsp;
intervene in the chaos but his son prince louis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:52.880
did not he invaded england at the head of a&nbsp;
french army of knights and mercenaries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:56.160
joined with the rebels at london&nbsp;
where they proclaimed him king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:00.480
the barons had decided that they would&nbsp;
rather be ruled by a foreign king than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:05.600
by john any longer despite being excommunicated&nbsp;
by the pope louis pressed the war against john&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:10.320
to a fever pitch plunging the entire kingdom into&nbsp;
anarchy and violence king john seemed to be at war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:14.480
with everyone his barons the french the scottish&nbsp;
who took advantage of the chaos to invade northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:19.120
england still john had plenty of money saved from&nbsp;
his various taxation schemes enough to pay for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:24.560
sizeable royal army john would attempt to fight&nbsp;
all of his enemies at once rather than compromise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:30.080
he was defiant to the last but in the midst of&nbsp;
this even his body betrayed him in september&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:35.440
1216 while long campaign john contracted&nbsp;
dysentery known at the time as the bloody flux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:40.160
the same disease that killed john's brother&nbsp;
henry in 1183 and john grew gravely ill as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:45.440
likely dehydrated due to severe diarrhea in&nbsp;
october the king was no longer able to travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:51.280
he was at newark castle in nottinghamshire on&nbsp;
the 19th of october 1216 when he died at age 49.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:56.320
one wonders how he might have felt as he laid&nbsp;
dying his once prosperous kingdom brought to ruin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.320 --> 00:21:00.480
his continental empire lost and england&nbsp;
overrun with foreign troops but really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:06.880
he had no one to blame but himself his selfishness&nbsp;
his paranoia and his tyranny had brought all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:15.840
this misfortune upon him it would be left to&nbsp;
his successors to attempt to clean up his mess

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:24.320
john's death proved a turning point in the&nbsp;
war with prince louis most of the rebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:29.680
barons switched allegiances from louis to john's&nbsp;
nine-year-old son who was crowned king henry iii&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:33.680
louis suffered a string of defeats over the next&nbsp;
year that compelled him to return to france and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:37.760
give up his claim to the english throne henry&nbsp;
would spend decades attempting to recapture the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:42.240
lost territories in normandy with no more success&nbsp;
than his father had the loss of normandy marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:47.120
a turning point in european history the beginning&nbsp;
of the historic rivalry between england and france&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:51.760
that would reach its heights during the hundred&nbsp;
years war from now on kings of england would rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:56.320
from england instead of from france and over the&nbsp;
course of the next century english would supplant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.320 --> 00:22:00.400
french as the language spoken at the english&nbsp;
court but it would be the magna carta that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:06.720
prove to be the lasting legacy of john's reign the&nbsp;
failed peace treaty was revived by henry's regency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:11.360
government as a framework for governing england&nbsp;
the charter would be reissued several times during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:16.960
henry's long reign and his successors would either&nbsp;
rule according to its precepts or face resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:22.480
from the nobles in particular from now on whenever&nbsp;
king wanted to levy attacks usually to fund a war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:27.840
he needed to ask parliament for it thus giving the&nbsp;
nobles and later the commons a stake in running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:32.560
the kingdom no one realized it at the time but the&nbsp;
great charter would prove to be the foundational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:37.600
document for almost every democratic constitution&nbsp;
the world over not that john should receive any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:41.600
credit for this of course the only reason the&nbsp;
magna carta was conceived in the first place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:46.800
was because john was such a bad king that the&nbsp;
nobles wanted to prevent someone as bad as him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:52.240
from running wild throughout england ever again&nbsp;
perhaps the greatest testament to how terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:57.440
john's reign really was is demonstrated by the&nbsp;
royal family themselves over the course of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:02.960
centuries there have been eight kings named henry&nbsp;
six georges eight edwards even two elizabeths&nbsp;&nbsp;

